              <li><a href="#record">Recording the layers</a></li>
              <li><a href="#edit">Trimming and looping</a></li>
              <li><a href="#map">Mapping into Sampler</a></li>
              <li><a href="#roundrobin">Where round-robin broke</a></li>
              <li><a href="#ship">Making it portable</a></li>
            </ol>
          </nav>
        </details>

        <div class="prose" data-od-id="article-body">

          <h2 id="plan">Decide the instrument before you record</h2>
          <p>
            The most expensive mistake in sampling is recording first. Every decision
            downstream — how many velocity layers, how far apart the sampled pitches sit,
            whether the release tail is captured separately — changes what you need to
            play into the microphone, and none of it can be added afterwards without
            going back to the room.
          </p>
          <p>
            For this build the target was narrow on purpose: a sustained viola patch that
            holds up under a slow legato line, not a full articulation set. That single
            constraint removed spiccato, pizzicato and the entire question of transition
            samples, and brought the session down from two days to one afternoon.
          </p>

          <figure class="fig">
            <div class="ph">
              <span class="ph__label"><strong>Screenshot</strong> — the planning sheet: pitches, layers, takes<br>to be captured at 2560 × 1600</span>
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              The sheet the session was run from. Anything not on it was not recorded,
              which is the point.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2 id="record">Recording the layers</h2>
          <p>
            Three dynamic layers is the smallest number that still reads as an instrument
            rather than a sample. Two layers produce an audible step at the crossover;
            four rarely earns its editing time for a sustain-only patch.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Record one full chromatic pass per dynamic, rather than all dynamics per note. Players hold a dynamic far more consistently across pitches than across repeats.</li>
            <li>Sample every minor third and let Sampler stretch the two semitones between. Beyond a minor third the formant shift starts to sound like a detuned copy.</li>
            <li>Leave four seconds of tail running after each note. The release is the part you cannot synthesise.</li>
            <li>Record a slate before each pass. Three hours later it is the only reliable way to tell layer two from layer three.</li>
          </ol>

          <figure class="audio" aria-label="Audio example, not yet recorded">
            <div class="audio__row">
              <button class="audio__btn" type="button" disabled aria-label="Play example — not yet available">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path d="M5 3.5l7 4.5-7 4.5V3.5z" fill="currentColor"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
              <div class="audio__wave" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            </div>
            <p class="audio__meta">
              Audio example — dry viola sustain, C3, layer 2 of 3.
              Not yet recorded; this slot stays empty until the session audio exists.
            </p>
          </figure>

          <h2 id="edit">Trimming and looping</h2>
          <p>
            A sustain loop that breathes is worth more than a loop that is technically
            seamless. Zero-crossing alignment removes the click; it does not remove the
            sense that the same second of bow is repeating. Crossfading a longer window
            across a slightly irregular section is what makes it stop sounding looped.
          </p>

          <figure class="settings">
            <figcaption>Sampler → Zone editor, final state</figcaption>
<pre><b>Velocity zones</b>    3      1–42 · 43–96 · 97–127
<b>Zone fade</b>         6      velocity steps, both edges
<b>Loop mode</b>         Forward, crossfade 40 ms
<b>Loop window</b>       1.4 s starting 0.9 s after attack
<b>Root key</b>          from filename, chromatic every 3 st
<b>Release</b>           1.8 s, sample tail retained
<b>Interpolation</b>     Good  (Best only on the render)</pre>
          </figure>

          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>The 40 ms crossfade is not a default worth keeping.</strong>
              On the lowest layer it smeared the bow change badly enough to hear. Layer
              one ended up at 90 ms while layers two and three stayed at 40 — Sampler
              allows the value per zone, and there is no reason to keep it uniform.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 id="map">Mapping into Sampler</h2>
          <p>
            Sampler reads root keys from filenames if the naming is consistent, which is
            the entire argument for deciding the naming scheme before the session rather
            than during the import. Files named
            <code style="font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.9em">vla_C3_v2_t1.wav</code>
            dropped into the zone editor in one pass; the earlier take, named by date,
            took twenty minutes to sort by hand.
          </p>

          <h2 id="roundrobin">Where round-robin broke</h2>
          <p>
            Two problems, both worth stating because neither is documented clearly
            anywhere we could find.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first: with round-robin selection set to random, repeated notes
            occasionally drew the same take twice, which defeats the purpose. Cycling
            through takes in order sounds less clever and behaves better — the ear notices
            a repeat far more readily than it notices a pattern.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second: the round-robin index resets when the device is reloaded, so the
            first note of every session came from the same take. Audible only when the
            patch opens a piece. Recording the takes at marginally different bow positions
            made the reset inaudible without touching the mapping at all.
          </p>

          <h2 id="ship">Making it portable</h2>
          <p>
            Sampler references the sample files by path. Without
            <strong>Collect All and Save</strong> the instrument works perfectly on the
            machine it was built on and nowhere else — the failure this publication's
            <a href="/publishing-roadmap/">DAW project guidelines</a> exist to prevent.
          </p>

          <section class="sources">
            <h2>Sources</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Ableton, <a href="https://www.ableton.com/en/live-manual/12/sampler/" rel="noopener">Live 12 manual — Sampler</a>. Consulted August 2026.</li>
              <li>Session notes and recordings from the build described above. Raw takes retained, not published.</li>
            </ol>
          </section>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

